1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:00,909
Welcome.

2
00:00:00,959 --> 00:00:04,960
In this video we walk through the Product Monitoring Book.

3
00:00:05,010 --> 00:00:07,685
It is a BOST daily BDC transfer report.

4
00:00:07,735 --> 00:00:11,527
We will cover what the workbook is designed to produce,

5
00:00:11,577 --> 00:00:16,347
how the Combined sheet is built, how we cleaned and checked the data,

6
00:00:16,397 --> 00:00:19,071
and finally what the analysis tells us.

7
00:00:20,321 --> 00:00:23,121
The workbook has 742 daily sheets.

8
00:00:23,171 --> 00:00:26,475
Each one is a copy of the same template.

9
00:00:26,525 --> 00:00:29,493
The report date is typed in cell G3.

10
00:00:29,543 --> 00:00:33,516
Below it is an Excel table that lists every BDC,

11
00:00:33,566 --> 00:00:39,133
which means bulk distribution company, against five volume columns.

12
00:00:39,183 --> 00:00:43,241
KSI is the Kumasi depot, then Buipe and Akosombo.

13
00:00:43,291 --> 00:00:45,923
AGO is diesel and PMS is petrol.

14
00:00:45,973 --> 00:00:52,211
The date column simply copies cell G3, and a total row adds up each column.

15
00:00:53,461 --> 00:00:57,124
The Summary sheet holds a pivot table.

16
00:00:57,174 --> 00:01:04,355
It shows total volume per BDC for the five columns, with filters for date,

17
00:01:04,405 --> 00:01:05,821
year and month.

18
00:01:05,871 --> 00:01:12,173
Under the pivot, GETPIVOTDATA formulas give the headline numbers.

19
00:01:12,223 --> 00:01:20,088
For example, Kumasi lifting is 2,328,784,800, and AGO liftings are 1,713,293,500.

20
00:01:20,138 --> 00:01:27,515
So the workbook answers: how much moved, through which depot, which product,

21
00:01:27,565 --> 00:01:29,274
and for which BDC.

22
00:01:30,524 --> 00:01:35,506
The Dashboard sheet has two pivot charts built on the same pivot table.

23
00:01:35,556 --> 00:01:39,333
A bar chart compares each BDC across the five columns,

24
00:01:39,383 --> 00:01:42,451
and a doughnut chart shows each BDC's share.

25
00:01:43,701 --> 00:01:46,538
So how is the Combined sheet built?

26
00:01:46,588 --> 00:01:50,579
Not with VLOOKUP, copy and paste, or Consolidate.

27
00:01:50,629 --> 00:01:54,043
The Combined sheet has no formulas at all.

28
00:01:54,093 --> 00:01:57,012
It is a Power Query called July2020.

29
00:01:57,062 --> 00:02:02,126
Step one, Excel.CurrentWorkbook lists every table in the file.

30
00:02:02,176 --> 00:02:06,580
Step two, it filters out the BDC list, the print area,

31
00:02:06,630 --> 00:02:09,301
and the query's own output table.

32
00:02:09,351 --> 00:02:12,105
Step three, it expands the tables,

33
00:02:12,155 --> 00:02:16,642
which stacks all 742 daily tables on top of each other.

34
00:02:16,692 --> 00:02:21,838
Finally it removes total rows and blank dates, sets data types,

35
00:02:21,888 --> 00:02:24,889
and adds Year and Month Name columns.

36
00:02:26,139 --> 00:02:32,456
The query loads its result to the Combined sheet: 14,943 rows in table July2020.

37
00:02:32,506 --> 00:02:37,550
That table feeds the pivot, the pivot feeds the Summary numbers,

38
00:02:37,600 --> 00:02:40,415
and the Summary feeds the Dashboard.

39
00:02:40,465 --> 00:02:45,111
Important: it only updates when someone clicks Refresh All.

40
00:02:46,361 --> 00:02:49,068
Before analysing, we validated.

41
00:02:49,118 --> 00:02:54,316
We compared Combined with the daily sheets, value by value.

42
00:02:54,366 --> 00:02:58,141
On all 729 shared dates they match exactly.

43
00:02:58,191 --> 00:03:04,990
But Combined also holds 2,438 rows from January to September 2020 whose daily

44
00:03:05,040 --> 00:03:07,036
sheets no longer exist.

45
00:03:07,086 --> 00:03:13,975
And it is missing the last two sheets, the fourth and fifth of September 2023,

46
00:03:14,025 --> 00:03:15,398
worth 7,618,500.

47
00:03:15,448 --> 00:03:22,158
The pivot was last refreshed on the morning of the fourth of September 2023.

48
00:03:23,408 --> 00:03:25,800
Here is the data quality log.

49
00:03:25,850 --> 00:03:27,821
We ran seventeen checks.

50
00:03:27,871 --> 00:03:33,379
All BDC names match the master list, there are no negative values,

51
00:03:33,429 --> 00:03:35,737
and every total row adds up.

52
00:03:35,787 --> 00:03:42,389
We did find problems: one duplicate row, one stray backtick in a number column,

53
00:03:42,439 --> 00:03:48,705
a trailing space in Lemla Petroleum, and 43 sheet names that end in spaces.

54
00:03:48,755 --> 00:03:53,758
After cleaning we have 14,984 rows covering 916 report days.

55
00:03:55,008 --> 00:03:57,154
The biggest problem is the date.

56
00:03:57,204 --> 00:04:00,449
This sheet is named the third of September 2021,

57
00:04:00,499 --> 00:04:03,676
but cell G3 still says the second of September.

58
00:04:03,726 --> 00:04:07,245
The sheet was copied and the date was never updated.

59
00:04:07,295 --> 00:04:12,051
Nine sheets have this problem, so their volume lands on the wrong day.

60
00:04:12,101 --> 00:04:14,453
We used the date in the sheet name,

61
00:04:14,503 --> 00:04:19,053
and kept the original date in a separate column so nothing is lost.

62
00:04:20,303 --> 00:04:22,222
Now the analysis.

63
00:04:22,272 --> 00:04:30,102
In total, 3,065,382,800 moved, or about 3.35 million per report day.

64
00:04:30,152 --> 00:04:35,084
Diesel is 56 percent and petrol 44 percent.

65
00:04:35,134 --> 00:04:42,036
Kumasi carries 76.2 percent of all volume, Buipe 19 percent,

66
00:04:42,086 --> 00:04:44,933
and Akosombo 4.8 percent.

67
00:04:46,183 --> 00:04:54,326
By year, the petrol share falls from 46.6 percent in 2020 to 38 percent in 2023.

68
00:04:54,376 --> 00:05:02,622
Buipe transfers drop sharply, from 246.8 million in 2020 to 89.7 million in 2022.

69
00:05:02,672 --> 00:05:07,743
Remember 2023 only runs to the fifth of September.

70
00:05:08,993 --> 00:05:11,079
Volume is concentrated.

71
00:05:11,129 --> 00:05:17,023
The top five BDCs, BOST, Juwel, Goenergy, Blue Ocean and Cirrus,

72
00:05:17,073 --> 00:05:20,088
move 68.4 percent of all product.

73
00:05:20,138 --> 00:05:23,618
BOST and Juwel alone are 38.9 percent.

74
00:05:23,668 --> 00:05:29,654
At the other end, seven BDCs each moved less than half a percent.

75
00:05:30,904 --> 00:05:36,774
Because the number of report days changes from month to month,

76
00:05:36,824 --> 00:05:39,924
we measure volume per report day.

77
00:05:39,974 --> 00:05:44,984
It was 3.57 million in 2020 and 3.64 million in 2021.

78
00:05:45,034 --> 00:05:49,280
It fell 18.1 percent to 2.98 million in 2022,

79
00:05:49,330 --> 00:05:54,149
then recovered 4.7 percent to 3.12 million in 2023.

80
00:05:55,399 --> 00:05:57,646
One month stands out.

81
00:05:57,696 --> 00:06:02,678
In June 2023, diesel volume fell to 6,142,500,

82
00:06:02,728 --> 00:06:08,694
compared with 53,206,500 in May and 44,990,000 in July.

83
00:06:08,744 --> 00:06:15,913
Diesel was zero on 14 of 21 report days, while petrol kept moving.

84
00:06:15,963 --> 00:06:22,039
This needs an explanation before anyone reads the trend.

85
00:06:23,289 --> 00:06:26,200
The customer mix is changing.

86
00:06:26,250 --> 00:06:32,941
BOST moved 285,980,000 in 2023, which is 52.1 percent of the year,

87
00:06:32,991 --> 00:06:36,208
up from 10 to 15 percent before.

88
00:06:36,258 --> 00:06:44,072
Meanwhile Blue Ocean fell from 135.2 million in 2021 to 19.3 million in 2023,

89
00:06:44,122 --> 00:06:50,404
and Chase Petroleum and Lemla Petroleum moved nothing in 2023.

90
00:06:51,654 --> 00:06:52,528
To sum up.

91
00:06:52,578 --> 00:06:55,669
One: volume depends on a few BDCs,

92
00:06:55,719 --> 00:07:01,491
so track the top five share and plan ahead with BOST and Juwel.

93
00:07:01,541 --> 00:07:09,161
Two: report trends per report day, and investigate June 2023 and the fall at Buipe.

94
00:07:09,211 --> 00:07:13,689
Three: find out why private BDCs are moving less,

95
00:07:13,739 --> 00:07:17,940
and add an alert for 30 days with no movement.

96
00:07:17,990 --> 00:07:20,527
Four: the Summary was stale.

97
00:07:20,577 --> 00:07:27,550
The corrected total is 3,065,382,800, against 3,057,764,300 in the original.

98
00:07:27,600 --> 00:07:31,431
Refresh on open and show the refresh date.

99
00:07:31,481 --> 00:07:37,438
Five: stop copied dates by deriving the date from the sheet name,

100
00:07:37,488 --> 00:07:40,025
or use one data entry table.

101
00:07:41,275 --> 00:07:47,201
Everything is in the project folder: the analysis workbook with live formulas and a

102
00:07:47,251 --> 00:07:52,241
reconciliation sheet, a README summary, and this video with subtitles.

103
00:07:52,291 --> 00:07:54,977
The original workbook was not changed.

104
00:07:55,027 --> 00:07:56,417
Thanks for watching.
